## 3. One Global Coefficient Correction

For either graph, restriction of the 249 eta9 coefficient directions gives
32 rows over

```text
k = F_101[gamma]/(M).
```

The two maps have ranks

```text
rank L_Gamma1 = 26,
rank L_Gamma2 = 26.
```

Stack them in first-H, second-H order:

```text
L_12 : k^249 -> k^64.
```

Exact elimination gives

```text
rank L_12 = 52,
nullity L_12 = 197,
cokernel dimension = 12.
```

The combined 64-row defect of the fixed coefficient lift satisfies all twelve
compatibility relations. RREF in the displayed basis, with all free variables
set to zero, emits a correction supported on 52 coefficient slots.

## 4. Theorem

**Two-H First-Order Joint Graph/Coefficient Lift Theorem.** The selected
first-H degree-three Fermat map has the unique normalized lift displayed above
through `101^2`. The union of its eta9 coefficient-restriction derivative and
the selected second-H derivative has rank 52. The combined fixed-lift defect
lies in this image. Consequently there exists one global eta9 coefficient
correction `delta_F` such that

```text
F_joint = F_Q + 101 delta_F mod 101^2
```

restricts to zero on both unchanged selected primitive-H graph maps. The
emitted canonical correction verifies all 64 rows by direct substitution.

**Proof.** The first map identities follow from the exact Mobius pullback,
the two full-rank scalar Hensel systems and direct reduction by the lifted
sheet equation. Restrict each displayed eta9 coefficient basis vector to each
graph and row-reduce the resulting `64 x 249` matrix over the selected residue
field. Exact RREF gives rank 52 and a consistent augmented system. Substituting
the emitted correction into the original equation over the completion ring
makes all 32 first-H and all 32 second-H coefficients zero modulo `101^2`.
QED.

## 5. Meaning

G3AF is stronger than two independent repairs. The same global coefficient
vector works for both graph components. The rank additivity

```text
26 + 26 = 52
```

also shows that the two selected primitive-H graphs impose independent linear
conditions at this order. Their shared solution still has 197 affine
directions, so compatibility is nonempty but physical selection is not yet
unique.

The 197-dimensional kernel is deformation freedom in this finite incidence
problem. It is not a count of physical fit parameters. The RREF convention is
a reproducible witness rule, not an MTT selection axiom.

